<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Fudayk</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. 693</span> · <a href="/centuries/7th-century">7th century</a> · Also known as Abd Allah ibn Thawr</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Fudayk, Abd <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/allah">Allah</a> ibn Thawr, was a Kharijite leader of the Najdiyya movement in eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arabia">Arabia</a>. Around 691 the movement's followers deposed and killed their leader Najda ibn <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/amir">Amir</a> and turned to him, and he ruled from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bahrain">Bahrain</a>. After Abd al-Malik reunited the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/umayyad-caliphate">Umayyad Caliphate</a>, an army from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basra">Basra</a> defeated and killed him in 693, ending the Najdiyya state.</p></div>
